number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|One administrative path can compromise a bridge and delete its recovery copies|The same lower-trust principal or verified control chain can control a hybrid bridge AND delete or invalidate all recovery copies required by the organization's recovery policy.|Host control path; backup catalog coverage; delete and retention-change rights; immutable/independent-copy state; recovery requirement.|Separate backup administration and retain independently protected recovery copies.|Seeing one mutable backup does not prove all copies are mutable. Incomplete backup inventory means Unknown.|T1490|AC-5;CP-9;CP-10|Preserve recoverability outside the administrative path affecting the primary system.||High
002|All usable tenant emergency access depends on the affected on-premises identity service|An assessed federation or synchronization outage/compromise scenario affects every approved emergency account's authentication dependency and no independently authenticated account meets the documented requirement.|Emergency-account inventory; authentication authority; role state; credential custody attestation; last authorized validation; dependency map.|Maintain protected independent cloud emergency access and validate it through the organization's approved procedure.|An undocumented inventory is Unknown, not proof no account exists. The collector never signs in as an emergency account.|T1531|CP-2;CP-10;AC-2|Retain access needed to recover when the normal identity dependency is unavailable.||High
003|Cloud backup administration is inherited from an exposed synced group|A source-group control path reaches a cloud backup or vault role capable of deleting recovery data for hybrid identity assets.|Source membership authority; verified synced group; exact vault/resource role; delete capability; protected assets and retention controls.|Separate recovery-data administration and protect the source of privileged backup group membership.|A generic cloud role name does not prove vault deletion rights. Resource scope and enforced retention must be evaluated.|T1098.007;T1490|AC-6;CP-9;AC-3|Protect cloud-held recovery data from lower-trust on-premises group authority.||High
004|A documented hybrid recovery plan has no current end-to-end validation evidence|An approved recovery requirement includes AD plus a specific bridge or federation dependency AND the required recovery test is overdue or explicitly failed.|Approved recovery scope and cadence; dated exercise record; dependency list; recorded outcome and owner attestation.|Test the end-to-end recovery sequence in an isolated authorized environment and resolve recorded failures.|This is an assisted/manual assurance check. No discovered test document is Unknown until the responsible owner confirms coverage.|T1490|CP-4;CP-10|Validate that the recovery measures can restore the complete identity dependency chain.||Medium
005|Required bridge configuration backups are missing from the recovery chain|An active hybrid service has an explicit configuration-backup requirement AND authoritative backup inventory confirms the required protected configuration is not retained.|Service inventory; approved recovery requirement; backup scope and retention metadata; encryption/custody information.|Include the supported service configuration in independently protected backups and document restore prerequisites.|Do not export live secrets into an assessment. Missing collector access to a backup platform does not prove no backup exists.|T1490|CP-9;CM-2;CP-10|Retain the configuration needed to reconstruct hybrid identity services.||High
006|Containment leaves a previously authorized cloud session usable beyond policy|An approved incident-containment record requires session revocation for a mapped identity AND authorized audit evidence shows continued relevant access after the supported revocation and propagation window.|Containment action timestamps; identity mapping; supported token/session behavior; relevant audit records; approved deadline.|Complete supported session and entitlement revocation and investigate the remaining authorization path.|Disabling AD does not revoke every cloud session. Do not replay tokens or test stolen credentials; use authorized audit evidence.|T1550.001;T1550.004|IR-4;AC-2;SC-23|Terminate affected authorization and verify containment using session-aware evidence.||High
007|Signing-key recovery shares the same exposed administrative boundary|A federation or cloud-trusted CA signing path is exposed AND the only documented recovery/rotation capability depends on identities or storage controlled by that same path.|Underlying key-use exposure; recovery custody and backup authorization; alternate operator evidence; approved rotation procedure.|Establish separately protected recovery custody and rehearse a supported signing-key rotation.|Key material is never collected. A missing custody document is Unknown until confirmed by the responsible owner.|T1649;T1606.002|SC-12;CP-9;CP-10|Maintain independent recovery of authentication-signing authority.||High
008|Provisioning control can block access needed to recover the tenant|A lower-trust provisioning operator can disable or remove the source objects of all documented recovery operators AND those operators lack independent supported cloud access.|Provisioning authority and scope; exact recovery identities; supported lifecycle flow; cloud role and authentication dependencies.|Separate recovery identities from ordinary provisioning authority and protect lifecycle changes.|Do not assume source deletion immediately removes all target identities. Supported flow, safeguards, and recovery-account coverage are prerequisites.|T1531;T1098|AC-5;AC-2;CP-2|Protect recovery access from the same lifecycle authority that can disrupt normal access.||High
